    "## Summary\n",
    "\n",
   |
 "| Section | Key Finding |\n",
    "|---------|-------------|\n",
    "| **Data** | 5,000 posts across TikTok, Instagram, YouTube (2022–2023) |\n",
    "| **Viral Label** | Viral posts were identified using the top 25% engagement score within each platform rather than one global threshold. This helped reduce platform bias since engagement levels differ between TikTok, Instagram, and YouTube. |\n",
    "| **Features** | The models used raw engagement metrics, along with temporal features such as Month, DayOfWeek, and IsWeekend, as well as categorical features such as Content Type, Region, and Hashtag usage.) |\n",
    "| **Leakage Issue** | Ratio-based features such as Engagement_Rate and Like_to_View were excluded from the final models because they were directly derived from the same engagement metrics used to create the viral label, which could have caused data leakage. |\n",
    "| **AUC Limitation** | The near-perfect AUC scores were likely influenced by the synthetic nature of the dataset, since the engagement values did not contain the same level of natural noise and unpredictability seen in real-world social media data. |\n",
    "| **Best Models** | Logistic Regression achieved the strongest overall ROC-AUC performance across all platforms, showing that the relationships between engagement metrics and virality were fairly consistent within this dataset. |\n",
    "| **Key Insight** | Platform-specific models performed better than using a single global model, suggesting that the factors driving virality differ across platforms. |\n",
    "| **Why LR Won** | The strong performance of Logistic Regression suggests that engagement metrics had relatively linear and separable relationships with virality within this dataset. |\n"
   ]
  }
